nodecode-caldav is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-event-read:

https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-event-read
commit 00e8aed782ad3d81e1fbc30abb202e3caf9a4dc0

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's two tools, list_events and read_event, are
here two tools of the same names with the same arguments, and the same calls
as Lisp verbs, caldav:list-events and caldav:read-event. They send the same
calendar-query REPORT the Python did -- the same filter body, Depth 1, the
credential as HTTP Basic, no redirect followed, a 2 MB ceiling on the answer
and a timeout on the request -- and read the multistatus the same way: the
root must be a multistatus and every inner status 200; a 200 is a failure
even when a calendar is in it. The iCalendar reading is the original's too:
unfolded lines, VEVENT counted at a BEGIN line, UID, SUMMARY, DTSTART,
DTEND, RECURRENCE-ID, RRULE and RDATE, a TZID kept as written and dropped for
a value ending in Z, one row per uid with a changed occurrence grouped under
its master and repeats said, up to 50 rows before the answer is cut. The
three environment variables became the cell's `caldav` section, which still
reads CALDAV_PASSWORD when no password is pasted there, and a section naming
none of them refuses the start where the plugin registered no tool.

What was left out: the cron watch. The plugin could be run by a Hermes cron
job that compared the window's uids with a saved set in plugin-data and
announced a uid that had moved into the window; that needed Hermes's own
execution identity, its write guard and two JSON state files, none of which a
Nodecode cell has to invent, because Nodecode's cron cell already runs a
prompt on a schedule -- so the two reads are here, without files, and a
schedule is the operator's to make. The plugin's skill (one line in the
skills index telling the model to look the tools up) is the cell's primer and
the two tool descriptions instead. The JSON envelope the Python answered --
ok, error, notify, truncated, next_step -- is the message itself here: a
failure signals with the status or the reason named, and a success answers
the events as lines, which is what a model reads.
